 and responsibility  commitment needs to be kept and  contracts are binding  advanced purchase agreements need to be  respected  today we have developed a system that  will allow us to know  whether vaccines are being exported from  the eu  this increased transparency will also  come with the responsibility for the eu  to authorize  with our member states these vaccine  exports  and we do this for three reasons firstly  to ensure that we can continue to work  on the basis of  evidence and complete information  secondly  to reinforce our capacity to deliver on  the advanced purchase agreements  so that citizens from the eu and beyond  can access vaccines and finally  to protect the integrity of our  substantial invest investments from the  eu  budget this is an insurance policy  in this endeavor our commitment to  global responsibility  is unwavering we will continue to  support  access to safe and effective vaccines  for all of our neighbors  and low and middle income countries  through kovacs  because this priority continues to be as  high as  as it has been from day one humanitarian  shipments and kovacs donations will not  be affected  nor will it affect our neighbors i also  want to be clear  about the fact that we commission member  states  and companies have done an exceptional  effort to develop  secure and distribute vaccines this  effort  will continue and must continue on all  sides and it depends on all parties  living to their contractual social and  moral  responsibilities today we take a step  in ensuring that accountability  continues to be  one of the key principles of our  commitment to citizens  thank you thank you evp and commissioner  we will take a few questions let me  start by jacob jacob you have the floor  hello uh yes i wanted to ask um  how uh this will work if there's a  disagreement between member states and  the commission  let's imagine a member state wants to  not  grant an authorization for an export and  the commission  wants to grant it who will have the last  word  and uh just to double check i think uh  you already said this but when this  enters into force so  this already passed the safeguards  committee thanks  yeah so on the first question how  this export authorization system will  work so it's for a member states  to prepare the decision on granting the  export authorization  and a commission is issuing an  opinion but it's unbinding opinion so to  quote from the  regulation the member states shall  decide on the request for authorization  in accordance with the commission's  opinion  well on the entry into force of the  regulation uh it's uh day following the  publication  in official journal stella i wanted to  add something  no i just want to say that this  this authorization mechanism is being  put into place to ensure transparency  which will be both in the benefit of  course  the member states uh because we are all  working in the same direction so  um i'm we're very confident that we will  have an agreement with the member states  on this because we're all working and  have been working together  in the same direction to ensure that we  have access to vaccines for all european  citizens and beyond  thank you thank you the next question  is for philip philippe you have the  floor  thank you uh phillip blankenship of  reuters you spelt out very clearly  um the exceptions in the words  humanitarian purposes and and the  kovacs recipients who obviously won't be  subject to this export control  but could you just give us what very  clearly what what are the conditions  under which  a an export authorization may be  rejected i mean  is it basically that the eu will look  uh to see that its um requirements be  met  and and subject to those being met then  accept  or reject the uh the the authorization  process  and and finally you said i mean it's  obviously factually correct that that  uh it will end to force the day after it  is published in the official journal and  i appreciate there's a process involved  here  but maybe you can kind of give an  indication of when it's likely to affect  to take effect i mean we're talking  about next week perhaps  as we have said uh from the beginning  and as i have  said and we have said from the beginning  that we are this  is in order to really protect the  advanced purchase agreements that we  have already signed  and which uh safeguard that we have the  availability of the vaccines that are  contained in this advanced purchase  agreement  so this would be the the main criteria  when we're  assessing the authorization mechanism  thank you  okay next one is alain allen please  [Music]  um  well uh as regards a list of exemptions  um there is a actually uh quite a  long list of exemptions of uh countries  um it also  includes after countries including  switzerland which  is specifically uh the question it  includes  countries in our neighborhood both  eastern  and southern neighborhood it includes  western balkans  and it includes countries  developing countries called by a corex  facility  if i'm not mistaken it's  some 92 countries  or or so so it's a long list of  countries which is  exempted well the measure is not  targeting any specific  country it's an export transparency and  authorization  measure to all certain countries which  are not  exempted  thank you for those questions i'll ask  just take  the first one first of all i want to say  something which i think is very  we have to be very clear and we have  said it many times before  uh we are not protecting ourselves  against any specific country and we're  not  in competition or in a race against any  country the only race we're in is  against this virus  and this is what is what has been in the  center of every single  uh piece of work that we've been doing  for now almost  uh it's almost a year so um  what we're doing through this mechanism  is protecting our apas  and through this uh assuring ensuring by  access of vaccines to the european  